  "query_preview": "..A persistent moist southerly flow aloft on the \nwestern flank of the upper ridge to our east will keep\nanomalously high PWAT values for September across the area \nthrough at least Thursday. There will be daily threats of\nrainfall rates between 1-2\"/hr, possibly higher, with some of\nthe stronger thunderstorms that develop this week leading to\nelevated chances of flash flooding each day through Thursday.\nWPC EROs for Days 1-4 (Mon-Thu) have slight risk for excessive\nrain each day across much of the forecast area. The potential \nwet week ahead may lead to some enhanced flows in washes, \ncreeks, and rivers that are already saturated.\n\nToday: Early morning satellite imagery showed considerable \ncloud cover across the eastern half of the forecast area with \nclear skies across western half of Pima county. There will be \nisolated weak, not long lived, showers popping up this morning. \nWith the increased threat for heavy rain and localized flash\nflooding from scattered showers and thunderstorms, a Flash \nFlood Watch is in effect for much of the area today from 11 am \nto 11 pm.\n\nTuesday: Areal coverage of storms likely down versus today.\n\nWednesday: A disturbance moving out of Sonora MX will bring a\nmore active day versus Tuesday.\n\nThursday: Not as active as Wednesday.\n\nFriday into this weekend: Drier air pushes in under westerly \nflow aloft, resulting in reduced precipitation chances. High \ntemperatures will be right around normal.\n\n8 to 14 day outlooks (Mon Sep 21 to Sun Sep 27)\nTemperature: Leaning above normal, 44-45% chance\nPrecipitation: Likely above, 49-52% chance",
